<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inanç Bey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Inanc Bey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inanç Bey was a Turkish frontier lord in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> who gave his name to the Inançid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denizli">Ladik</a> near ancient Laodicea, modern Denizli. The beylik lay on the trade road through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Meander</a> valley and was known for gold-embroidered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textiles described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>.</p></div>
